}}{{Politics of Peru}}

General elections were held in [[Peru]] on 8 April 1990, with a second round of the presidential elections on 10 June.<ref name=N1>[[Dieter Nohlen]] (2005) ''Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume II'', p454 {{ISBN|978-0-19-928358-3}}</ref> This exercise was to elect the [[President of the Republic]], two vice presidents, and the members of [[Congress of the Republic of Peru|Congress]]. The elections filled 180 seats in the Chamber of Deputies and 60 seats in the Senate for the 1990-1995 governmental period.

The run-off was between favorite, novelist [[Mario Vargas Llosa]] leading a coalition of economically liberal parties collectively known as the [[Democratic Front (Peru)|Democratic Front]] and political underdog [[Alberto Fujimori]] of the populist and more moderate [[Cambio 90]]. Vargas Llosa won the first round with a small plurality, but alienated much of the electorate with a comprehensive [[privatisation]] agenda, bolstering the allegedly unelectable Fujimori who had finished second ahead of [[Luis Alva Castro]] of the ruling [[American Popular Revolutionary Alliance|APRA]] party to enter the run-off against Vargas Llosa. Fujimori eventually won a landslide victory and would remain president for ten years until his forced resignation in November 2000.

==Background==
At the time of the 1990 elections the government of [[Alan García]] of the [[Peruvian Aprista Party]] (APRA) faced significant public disapproval. This disapproval was due to attempts to nationalize the banking system, purportedly to control rampant inflation that had been uncontrollable since 1987. Garcia's administration was also marred by corruption scandals and a resurgence of terrorist violence, notably from the terrorist organization [[Shining Path]].<ref name="Corteidh">{{cite web |title=Outsider Presidents and Neophyte Ministers: Evidence from the Fujimori Example |url=https://www.corteidh.or.cr/tablas/r32071.pdf |access-date=21 September 2024}}</ref> Consequently, it was anticipated that the elections would prompt a significant right-wing response.

==Presidential candidates==
[[Mario Vargas Llosa]], a widely recognized writer, ran as the candidate of the tripartite coalition [[Democratic Front (Peru)|Democratic Front]] (FREDEMO). Vargas Llosa was initially favored to win decisively, potentially eliminating the need for a runoff, particularly given the perceived weakness of the incumbent APRA party. APRA nominated [[Luis Alva Castro]], its second vice-president, as its candidate. However, as the campaign progressed, [[Alberto Fujimori]], representing the small new party [[Cambio 90]], began to gain increasing support in the polls. Fujimori's rise was attributed to his moderate profile,<ref name="Schmidt">{{cite journal |last1=Schmidt |first1=Gregory D |title=Fujimori's 1990 Upset Victory in Peru: Electoral Rules, Contingencies, and Adaptive Strategies |journal=Comparative Politics |date=April 1996 |volume=28 |issue=3 |pages=321–354 |doi=10.2307/422210 |jstor=422210 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/422210 |access-date=21 September 2024|url-access=subscription }}</ref> which drew support away from the traditional candidates.<ref name="Demons and Redeemers">{{cite web |title=1990 Elections: Demons and Redeemers in the New Peru |url=http://lanic.utexas.edu/project/laoap/iep/ddtlibro36.pdf |access-date=21 September 2024}}</ref><ref name="Annunciation">{{cite web |title=Fujimori's Annunciation: Alan Garcia 1985-1990 |url=http://lanic.utexas.edu/project/laoap/desco/desco00001.pdf |access-date=21 September 2024}}</ref><ref name="Comas">{{cite news |last1=Comas |first1=Jose |title='Chinito' Fujimori is hot on Vargas Llosa's heels |url=https://elpais.com/diario/1990/04/08/internacional/639525601_850215.html |access-date=21 September 2024 |work=el Pais |date=Apr 7, 1990}}</ref><ref name="Journal of Political Science">{{cite journal |title=Political Theory and History |journal=Journal of Political Science |volume=38 |page=1 |url=https://revcienciapolitica.com.ar/num38art5.php |access-date=21 September 2024}}</ref>

==Campaign==
During the presidential runoff Fujimori received endorsements from United Left and Socialist Left, along with a faction of the APRA led by García. Vargas Llosa was supported by [[Ricardo Belmont]], the newly elected mayor of Lima, and [[Ezequiel Ataucusi]], the leader of the [[Agricultural People's Front of Peru|Agricultural People's Front]]. Vargas Llosa also received backing from various media outlets and personalities, including the newspaper ''[[Expreso]]'', [[Panamericana Televisión]] and television host Augusto Ferrando.<ref name="Godoy">{{cite book |last1=Godoy |first1=Jose Alejandro |last2=Romero |first2=Ezequiel |title=The Last Dictator |date=2021 |publisher=Penguin |isbn=9786124272769 |pages=659 |url=https://dokumen.pub/el-ultimo-dictador-9786124272769.html |access-date=22 September 2024}}</ref>

==Debates==
A debate between Vargas Llosa and Fujimori took place on 3 June  1990 during the runoff campaign.<ref name="Comas 2">{{cite news |last1=Comas |first1=Jose |title=Side by side in Peru, despite Vargas Llosa's triumph over Fujimori on television |url=https://elpais.com/diario/1990/06/05/internacional/644536817_850215.html |access-date=21 September 2024 |work=el Pais |date=June 4, 1990}}</ref> This was the first televised debate in Peruvian presidential elections.<ref name="Garza Estrada">{{cite news |last1=de la Garza Estrada |first1=German |title=History of debates in Latin America |url=https://www.excelsior.com.mx/opinion/german-de-la-garza-estrada/historia-de-los-debates-en-america-latina/1234422 |access-date=21 September 2024 |work=Excelsior}}</ref><ref name="El Comercio">{{cite news |title=25 years after the first presidential debate in Peru |url=http://elcomercio.pe/blog/huellasdigitales/2015/06/a-25-anos-del-primer-debate-presidencial-en-el-peru |access-date=21 September 2024 |work=El Comercio |date=June 3, 2015}}</ref><ref name="The Debate">{{cite journal |last1=Vargas Llosa |first1=Mario |last2=Fujimori |first2=Alberto |title=The Debate: Complete Version of the Debate Held Between the Candidates for the Presidency of the Republic, at the Civic Center of Lima, on June 3, 1990 |url=http://repositorio.up.edu.pe/handle/11354/399 |journal=Repositorio de la Universidad del Pacífico - up |date=November 1990 |access-date=21 September 2024}}</ref>

