==Aftermath==
The two chambers of the elected Congress in Peru were unable to complete their constitutional terms due to a [[1992 Peruvian self-coup|self-coup]] by Fujimori on 5 April 1992, in which he dissolved Congress and intervened in the judiciary,<ref name="Wills">{{cite web |last1=Wills |first1=Maria Emma |last2=Pinto |first2=Maria Teresa |title=Peru's Failed Search for Political Stability (1968-2000) |url=https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/28248/ |access-date=22 September 2024}}</ref> effectively suspending the constitutional governance. Following the coup, Fujimori governed with full powers until the end of 1993, when a [[1992 Peruvian Democratic Constituent Congress election|Democratic Constituent Congress]] was inaugurated. Despite the political upheaval, Fujimori completed his term and was re-elected in [[1995 Peruvian general election|1995]]. This period of governance led to significant constitutional reform, including the abolition of the Senate. As a result, the 1990 elections were the last for the bicameral parliament, until it was restored in time for the [[2026 Peruvian general election|2026 presidential election]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Lopez Marina |first1=Diego |title=Peru’s Congress approves return to bicameralism after 30 years |url=https://perureports.com/perus-congress-approves-return-to-bicameralism-after-30-years/10363/ |website=Peru Reports |publisher=Espacio Media |access-date=11 June 2026}}</ref>

